Inheritance/default-ink probe, run 2026-09-23 as a throwaway fact in
tests/FluentJalium.Tests/AstraForegroundArrivalTests.cs (since removed; the reading it
established is the resident fact DefaultTextInkTracksTheVariant).

Shape: one ContentControl carrying a local magenta (#FFFF00FF) with a TextBlock passed
straight through it as Content, and a second ContentControl carrying the same magenta with
a string Content, so its label is generated by the presenter. Read both, then re-read after
FluentThemeManager.ApplyTheme(Dark).

  direct label reads #E4000000; generated label #FFFF00FF; carrier is #FFFF00FF;
  control carries #FFFF00FF. after Dark: direct label reads #FFFFFFFF;
  generated label #FFFF00FF; primary alias is #FFFFFFFF.

Read out:

- A label that nothing writes on is not inkless and not inherited: it carries #E4000000 in
  Light and #FFFFFFFF in Dark. This probe printed colours; the identity behind them - that
  those are the very instances TextFillColorPrimaryBrush holds in each variant - is what the
  resident fact DefaultTextInkTracksTheVariant now asserts with Assert.Same. So a row whose
  value forwards TextFillColorPrimaryBrush writes the colour the part would read anyway, and
  deleting it cannot be observed from the part - which is what the three deletion mutants in
  mut-bare-*/mut-carrier-*/mut-del-*.log kept showing green.
- The presenter path is the opposite: the generated label does follow the control, magenta in
  both variants. That is #51's split restated on a bare pair of controls rather than on a
  template.
